#
# Blocks ICMP timestamp requests and replies, mitigating CVE-1999-0524. The
# Linux kernel has no sysctl to suppress ICMP timestamp responses; upstream
# netdev maintainers have rejected four separate kernel patches (2015-2026)
# and consistently direct this fix to netfilter. Loaded by
# delphix-firewall.service (nft -f) before any network interface comes up.
#
# "add table"/"add chain" are no-ops on an object that already exists, and
# "flush chain" clears any rules a prior run already installed before this
# run's "add rule" re-adds them -- unlike a bare "table inet delphix { ... }"
# block (which errors "File exists" on a second run) or a leading
# "delete table inet delphix" (which errors on the very first boot, when
# the table doesn't exist yet to delete), this form is idempotent on every
# run -- first boot, a later reboot, or this unit's own ExecStop/ExecStart
# restart cycle -- with no risk of accumulating duplicate rules. It also
# never touches any table other than its own, unlike "flush ruleset".
#
add table inet delphix
add chain inet delphix input { type filter hook input priority filter - 10 ; }
add chain inet delphix output { type filter hook output priority filter - 10 ; }
flush chain inet delphix input
flush chain inet delphix output
add rule inet delphix input icmp type timestamp-request drop
add rule inet delphix output icmp type timestamp-reply drop
